/**
 * World session — the loadWorld/unloadWorld lifecycle.
 *
 * Single active world: navigation is deterministic unload → load.
 * `loadWorld` = fetch + parse (src/veml/loadWorld, which records
 * lastParseResult and never rejects for document problems).
 *
 * LOAD-THEN-SWAP (conformance F.14): the outgoing world is torn down ONLY
 * AFTER the target's fetch+parse SUCCEEDS. A failed navigation (a portal to an
 * unreachable URL, a Back/hash to a broken URL — any fetch/parse failure)
 * therefore LEAVES YOU IN THE CURRENT WORLD, and the error card paints over
 * it. Single active world is preserved: fetch+parse holds only bytes + a
 * parsed document — no second Scene ever coexists.
 *
 * On success, build a FRESH world Scene + camera +
 * first-person controller whose WalkableSource is the registry-backed
 * provider (registry-owned meshes ONLY; the controller is untouched, the seam
 * is its constructor argument). `unloadWorld` disposes every registry entity
 * (count → 0), tears the world scene down, and restores the no-world
 * test scene.
 *
 * The runtime stays EMBEDDABLE: this module owns the lifecycle;
 * main.ts is a thin consumer that renders whatever `active()` returns.
 *
 * World entry position: after the world is built, `resolveSpawn`
 * (src/scene/spawnResolve.ts) resolves the entry pose via the spawn chain
 * (environment/spawn → tag="spawn" → the `spawn` override → origin) with an auto-frame
 * fallback for off-origin no-spawn worlds; the controller is constructed AT the
 * resolved feet + yaw. Hold-until-first-grounded keeps the player
 * from falling while a glb floor streams in; grounding then owns the settle.
 *
 * Lighting: the world scene has NO placeholder light — the
 * world-owned lighting comes from the environment. ANY world with
 * zero declared `light` entities gets the deferred default lighting profile
 * (hemispheric + directional key, on top of the branch's own IBL/skybox/sun) so
 * its StandardMaterial primitives are never left black; a world that declares
 * its own lights is authoritative; those lights ride `environment.dispose()`.
 */
